{#
  The k3s section, longer than the others because a Kubernetes deployment needs two things done before `kubectl apply`
  that neither `mise` nor compose does: the images have to exist on the node, since manifests reference images rather
  than building them, and the credential has to be loaded as a `Secret`, since it is the one generated file the
  repository does not carry.

  Only where the hub comes from differs between the two image sources; everything else about producing the scenario
  image is the same either way. A published scenario's Dockerfile names the released hub image itself, so its build
  needs no hub of its own and no context to hand one over in.

  Where the build runs follows from what the image reads: a checkout when it copies anything out of one, and otherwise
  this directory. A build from this directory names no Dockerfile, since `Dockerfile` is docker's own default; one from
  the repository root has to name the scenario's. The token is said wherever the build runs: a scenario staging a
  mise-provisioned package builds from the repository root and can still fetch released modules, and then needs the
  token just the same. The secret's id and the variable it is read from go straight to the flag, since held in a shell
  variable of its own the pair reads to a secret scanner as a credential assigned in the clear. The template variable
  holding that flag is named for the registry rather than the secret for the same reason.
#}
{% set place = "From the repository root" if run.repository else "From this directory" %}
{% set location = place ~ ("," if run.registry_token else ":") %}
{% set build_contexts = run.build_contexts | map("quote") | flags("--build-context") %}
{% set hub_context = "" if published else ' --build-context "hub=docker-image://$hub"' %}
{% set registry_flag = " --secret id=" ~ token_secret ~ ",env=" ~ token_env if run.registry_token else "" %}
{% set dockerfile_flag = ' -f "$dockerfile"' if run.repository else "" %}
## Run With k3s

{% if published %}
The manifests reference images by name and never build them, so this scenario's own image has
to reach the node first. It layers its modules onto the released hub image, which its Dockerfile
names and the build pulls. {{ location }}
{% else %}
The manifests reference images by name and never build them, so build and import each one first.
This scenario's image layers its modules onto the module-less hub image and takes that hub as a
_named build context_ rather than building it, so the hub has to exist first -- a plain
`docker build` of the scenario Dockerfile fails on `FROM hub`. {{ location }}
{% endif %}
{% if run.registry_token %}
with `GITHUB_TOKEN` set to a token that can read the GitHub Packages the build fetches this
scenario's released modules from:
{% endif %}

```bash
scenario={{ name }}
{% if not published %}
hub=et-ws-server-hub:latest
{% endif %}
image="et-ws-server-$scenario:latest"
{% if run.repository %}
dockerfile="{{ run.dockerfile }}"
{% endif %}
{% if not published %}
docker build -t "$hub" -f services/ws-server/Dockerfile .
{% endif %}
docker build{{ hub_context }}{{ build_contexts }}{{ registry_flag }} -t "$image"{{ dockerfile_flag }} .
docker save "$image" | sudo k3s ctr images import -
```

